n have also jumped in  the mix Johnson Johnson is looking at  starting a clinical trial in November  Sanofi as estimated they can start a  clinical trial in early 2021 so those  are much longer timelines as far as  starting a clinical trial they kind of  envision a tortoise and a hare situation  where they have proven technology  they've done this many times they have  the resources of Big Pharma to pump out  this to a mass market of hundreds of  millions of people if needed  so those approaches are also worth  watching and they're moving as fast as  they can on that front vaccine  development is really expensive and with  infectious diseases it's hard to know  the return on investment for these  companies so there's big funding gap  with vaccine development that's remains  unanswered it's worth considering the  case of Merck one of the biggest drug  makers in the world they have the only  FDA approved Ebola vaccine that took  several years of clinical trials and on  president's scale Merck had to invest  hundreds of millions of dollars to get  this over the finish line and it's  essentially a net loss for the company  financially one group that's really  helped accelerate the pace of vaccine  development is called sepi the Coalition  for epidemic preparedness of innovations  this was started by the Bill and Melinda  Gates Foundation along with a lot of  other philanthropic support in 2017 it's  backed and funded the progression of  candidates including moderna x' as well  as several other vaccines in the early  stages there's a lot of unknowns  surrounding a corona virus vaccine not  just is it safe and effective but when  we get to that point or if we get to  that point with a vaccine candidate what  happens next how much data will the FDA  require could the FDA offer an emergency  approval  what would pricing and accessibility  look like particularly if a small  biotech like moderna or some of these  other small companies that are  progressing candidates into the